<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="dcdb0857"></attribute>
<attribute name="externalId" nodeId="bfe3defb"></attribute>
<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="51af6603">&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        The Development Team is required to attend each daily Scrum meeting.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        The Product Owner and Scrum Master are not required; however: 
        &lt;ul&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;
                The Scrum Master may still attend for the purpose of facilitation.
            &lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;
                The Product Owner may still attend only to understand the current status of the team&apos;s work.
            &lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;/ul&gt;
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        The daily Scrum meeting is intended to be an internal meeting for the Development Team. If others are present, the
        Scrum Master is responsible for&amp;nbsp;seeing that&amp;nbsp;they do not disrupt the meeting. 
        &lt;ul&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;
                Only those responsible for executing the actual work (Development Team) are allowed to actively participate
                during the meeting, all others should remain quiet.
            &lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;li&gt;
                In the event the team is new to the format of the meeting (e.g., lack of discipline, too many off topic
                conversations occurring, etc.), the Scrum Master may support the team by moderating the meeting.
            &lt;/li&gt;
        &lt;/ul&gt;
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Development Team members typically meet immediately following the daily Scrum meeting for follow on conversations
        or to further plan their work with the appropriate parties. This keeps the meeting on track by not involving
        unnecessary parties in&amp;nbsp;more&amp;nbsp;detailed and unrelated discussions.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Remember to update the Burndown Chart during the meeting if it was not updated beforehand&amp;nbsp;as work was
        completed
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="fc7ccfe7"></attribute>
<attribute name="usageGuidance" nodeId="ca7d0c44"></attribute>
<attribute name="refinedDescription" nodeId="d8327b16">&lt;p&gt;
    Schedule the&amp;nbsp;scrum&amp;nbsp;meeting to occur daily and invite the appropriate stakeholders, including all relevant
    members of the Development Team. Aim to keep the&amp;nbsp;time and location of the meeting the same for the sake of
    consistency. The meeting can either be virtual or in-person depending on the nature of the team (virtual or
    co-located). Time-box the meeting to 15 minutes and do not allow the duration to exceed this timeframe. A typical
    manner in which to conduct the meeting is to ask each member of the Development Team to answer the following 3
    questions:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        What did you do yesterday that helped the team move in the direction of achieving the sprint goal?
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        What will you do today to help the team move in the direction of achieving the sprint goal?
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Do you have any impediments that may prevent you from completing your work?
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The intent of these questions is to provide a common understanding by the Development Team of the current status so
    they can make informed decisions when planning for their next day of work. Other approaches for conducting this meeting
    are allowed as long as the approach aims to build common understanding within the team.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    It is important to make sure this meeting occurs daily and that the Development Team participates. The Development Team
    should actually be able to conduct the meeting themselves as facilitated by the Scrum Master. Ultimately, the
    Development Team needs to&amp;nbsp;gain a shared understanding of how they will collaborate each day to work toward
    achieving the sprint goal. &amp;nbsp;
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
</Element>
